# Analyzer Profiles

This distro reads its analyzer profiles from a two-layer tree under
`configs/analyzer-profiles/`. At runtime, Docker Compose mounts a merged
view that the contextualization CLI materializes from the two layers.

## Layout

```
configs/analyzer-profiles/
├── core/                  # upstream-managed defaults (committed)
│   ├── astm/
│   ├── file/
│   └── hl7/
├── distro/                # distro-specific overrides + additions (committed)
│   ├── astm/
│   ├── file/
│   └── hl7/
└── .active/               # materialized merged view (gitignored)
```

- **`core/`** holds the canonical, upstream-validated profiles. Currently
  vendored into the template; a future standalone
  `openelis-analyzer-profiles` repo will become the authoritative source,
  refreshed via `tools/contextualize/sync_analyzer_profiles.py`. Do not edit
  files in `core/` by hand — your changes will be lost when the next sync
  pulls upstream.
- **`distro/`** holds the profiles your deployment owns: overrides of upstream
  defaults (drop a file with the same path as a `core/` file) and additions
  (drop a file with a new name). Filename collision: `distro/` wins.
- **`.active/`** is the merged tree the OpenELIS container reads from. Never
  edit it directly. It is rebuilt by every `oe_context.py apply`.

## Workflow

After editing anything under `core/` or `distro/`:

```bash
python3 tools/contextualize/oe_context.py apply
```

`apply` rewrites `.active/` from `core/` overlaid by `distro/`. `doctor`
warns (not fails) when a filename appears in both layers, so you can see
which distro overrides are active.

On a fresh `copier copy`, the post-generate task materializes `.active/`
automatically — the demo stack boots with the upstream profile set without
any manual step. After that, the operator runs `apply` whenever they
change profile files.

## Adding a distro profile

1. Drop the JSON file at `configs/analyzer-profiles/distro/<protocol>/<name>.json`
   (where `<protocol>` is `astm`, `file`, or `hl7`).
2. Run `python3 tools/contextualize/oe_context.py apply` to refresh `.active/`.
3. `docker compose restart oe.openelis.org` to make OE re-read the mount.

## Overriding a core profile

Same workflow as adding, but use the exact same `<protocol>/<name>.json`
path as the file under `core/` you want to override. `doctor` will warn
that the override is in effect.
